WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.799 --> 00:00:03.100
BETTERHELP:
If you spend enough time on YouTube, I’m

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:04.770
sure you’ve heard of BetterHelp.

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:09.550
They’re the online therapy platform that’s
supposed to help people work through the darkest

00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:11.099
portions of their lives.

00:00:11.099 --> 00:00:14.030
And for the most part, they do a pretty good
job.

00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:19.029
They have managed to grow a massive network
of 23,000 fully certified, experienced therpaists

00:00:19.029 --> 00:00:22.000
who serve over 2.5 million people.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:26.830
And it seems that almost all of these users
are pretty happy given that they have over

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:30.820
a 100,000 reviews on the app store with an
average rating of 4.8.

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:33.790
But, it’s not all good news.

00:00:33.790 --> 00:00:39.070
You see, while BetterHelp’s overall goals
and intentions may be pure, they’re behavior

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:43.860
has largely been influenced by the need to
maximize profits and achieve global domination

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:46.940
as the go to mental therapy platform.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:50.950
For example, you’ve probabaly heard of doctor-patient
privilege.

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:54.190
This is one of the most important aspects
of therapy.

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:58.489
More times than not, people visit therapists
to open up about topics that their ashamed

00:00:58.489 --> 00:01:01.199
about or embarrassed about.

00:01:01.199 --> 00:01:04.720
Doctor-patient privilege is what encourages
these people to share these vulnerable moments

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:06.480
and get the help they need.

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:11.700
As such, protecting doctor-patient privilege
is of utmost importance for most clinics and

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:15.770
psychiatrists but this doesn’t seem to be
the case at BetterHelp.

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:21.840
In fact, BetterHelp has repeatedly sold sensitive
data to Facebook, Snapchat, Pinterest and

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:25.080
other such platforms over the past several
years.

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:31.020
But the worst part though was how they addressed
the situation when the FTC confronted them

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:33.580
about this practice earlier this year.

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:37.670
The normal response would’ve been to either
deny that they were selling this data or to

00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:40.369
issue a public apology for their behavior.

00:01:40.369 --> 00:01:45.409
But BetterHelp actually had the audacity to
claim that yes, they were selling data, but

00:01:45.409 --> 00:01:47.780
no it’s not wrong.

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:51.759
They would write off the situation as quote
“industry-standard practice...

00:01:51.759 --> 00:01:55.909
routinely used by some of the largest health
providers, health systems, and healthcare

00:01:55.909 --> 00:01:56.920
brands."

00:01:56.920 --> 00:02:02.330
It seems that BeterHelp has made their position
clear and that’s just the tip of the iceberg.

00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:07.170
So, join me as we take a look at the dark
side of BetterHelp.

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:17.440
SELLING OUT:
Usually, when we’re talking about data privacy,

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:21.110
it’s more of an ethical question as opposed
to a legal one.

00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:26.030
For example, Google and Meta’s data collection
is questionable from an ethical perspective

00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:28.269
but not from a legal one.

00:02:28.269 --> 00:02:32.180
After all, they lay out exactly what they’re
gonna collect in their privacy policy and

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:36.880
you basically have to sign off this agreement
to even use most of these platforms.

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:40.250
With BetterHelp on the other hand, that’s
not the case.

00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:44.600
Now, I’m sure that if you really dig into
their fine print, somewhere it acknowledges

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:49.830
that they may share your data, but throughout
the platform, they constantly reassure customers

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:51.930
that privacy is a top priority.

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:56.080
“Rest assured – any information provided
in this questionnaire will stay private between

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:57.870
you and your counselor.”

00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:02.190
Messages with your Therapist are not shared
with any Third Party, and your live sessions

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:04.050
are not recorded.

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:08.590
We also do not share when you send a message,
or have a session with your Therapist, with

00:03:08.590 --> 00:03:10.159
any Third Party.

00:03:10.159 --> 00:03:15.390
This last statement must’ve been a new addition
because they used to do exactly this.

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:20.950
Back in 2020, an investigation revealed that
BetterHelp was allegedly sharing the metadata

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:23.940
of therapist conversations with Facebook.

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:28.909
Facebook was being alerted everytime a user
opened BetterHelp and sent a message.

00:03:28.909 --> 00:03:33.459
This meant that Facebook was essentially getting
a full log of when users were having therapy

00:03:33.459 --> 00:03:38.270
sessions, where they were attending sessions
from, and for how long.

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:42.870
When confronted about this, BetterHelp would
simply decline to answer.

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:47.890
You would think that they would stop selling
data after this confrontation but it seems

00:03:47.890 --> 00:03:50.709
that they may have actually doubled down.

00:03:50.709 --> 00:03:55.900
According to an FTC report that came out in
March of 2023, BetterHelp allegedly used a

00:03:55.900 --> 00:04:01.230
wide variety of tactics to share the health
information of over 7 million consumers with

00:04:01.230 --> 00:04:03.680
Facebook, Snapchat, and Pinterest.

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:07.930
They even allegedly shared data collected
from health questionnaires which included

00:04:07.930 --> 00:04:09.600
questions like these.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:13.220
Are you experiencing overwhelming sadness,
grief, or depression?

00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:16.530
Have you had thoughts that you would be better
off not alive?

00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:20.060
And, have you taken medication or been in
therapy before.

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:22.560
Why would BetterHelp share this data you ask?

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:27.759
Well, so that these users could be retargeted
with BetterHelp ads.

00:04:27.759 --> 00:04:32.070
According to the FTC complaint, “Using this
health information for advertising, [BetterHelp]

00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:35.760
has brought in hundreds of thousands of new
users, resulting in millions of dollars in

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:36.990
additional revenue.”

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:41.490
This by the way seems to be working out really
well for BetterHelp as they just had their

00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:43.830
first billion dollar year.

00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:49.460
And the fine for all of this is just a mere
$7.8 million which will be used to refund

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:50.910
affected users.

00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:55.229
As we previously mentioned, BetterHelp never
admitted guilt claiming that this was just

00:04:55.229 --> 00:04:59.870
standard industry practice and unfortunately,
they’re actually right.

00:04:59.870 --> 00:05:04.900
Around the same time that BetterHelp was exposed,
it came out that another online therapy platform,

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:06.370
Cerebral, had done the same thing.

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:11.000
They had shared the private health information,
including metal health assessemnts, of 3.1

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:14.930
million patients with Facebook, Google, and
TikTok.

00:05:14.930 --> 00:05:19.640
And according to a report from Mozilla, it’s
the same case with most other online therapy

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:20.830
platforms as well.

00:05:20.830 --> 00:05:25.800
As such, the FTC is more concerned about reforming
the entire industry as opposed to just fining

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:28.270
BetterHelp for as much as possible.

00:05:28.270 --> 00:05:33.600
And in that vein, they’ve proposed a couple
of new guidelines and reforms.

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:43.060
MENTAL HEALTH REFORM:
The FTC has proposed a total of 6 new reforms

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:48.020
that BetterHelp must abide by and other platforms
should probably follow as well.

00:05:48.020 --> 00:05:52.160
The first reform is expanding the definition
of health information.

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:55.800
One of the main reasons that these platforms
are able to get away with sharing so much

00:05:55.800 --> 00:06:01.200
information about users is because a lot of
like email addresses, ip addresses, and meta

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:04.650
data isn’t technically considered health
information.

00:06:04.650 --> 00:06:08.169
But, the FTC is arguing that context counts.

00:06:08.169 --> 00:06:13.380
Sure, an email address may not usually be
considered health information, but when a

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:19.110
consumer enters an email address on a mental
health platform, it’s a different story.

00:06:19.110 --> 00:06:23.979
That email address suddenly becomes personally
identifiable information that this user may

00:06:23.979 --> 00:06:25.139
need therapy.

00:06:25.139 --> 00:06:30.040
So, moving forward, these platforms should
treat auxiliary information as health information

00:06:30.040 --> 00:06:31.080
as well.

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:35.630
The second reform centers on impleneitng policies,
practices, and procedures to protect health

00:06:35.630 --> 00:06:36.650
information.

00:06:36.650 --> 00:06:41.070
This one is pretty straight forward, the FTC
claims that BetterHelp doesn’t have written

00:06:41.070 --> 00:06:45.330
policies and procedures for protecting the
privacy of health information.

00:06:45.330 --> 00:06:49.460
They also fell short on properly training
and supervising employees that had access

00:06:49.460 --> 00:06:50.460
to health information.

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:56.889
As such, platforms must implement the required
policies and procedures but more importantly,

00:06:56.889 --> 00:07:02.550
platforms must get affirmative express consent
before sharing user data with 3rd parties.

00:07:02.550 --> 00:07:07.389
Speaking of transparency, the FTC has also
ruled that these platforms must ditch their

00:07:07.389 --> 00:07:09.030
deceptive designs.

00:07:09.030 --> 00:07:13.890
One of the main reasons that people trust
these platforms so easily is because the platforms

00:07:13.890 --> 00:07:14.940
make statements like this.

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:18.759
“We never sell or rent any information you
share with us.”

00:07:18.759 --> 00:07:22.570
Reading this, it’s natural to think that
your private data is safe.

00:07:22.570 --> 00:07:27.780
It’s not until you read the fine print that
you actually understand what’s going on.

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:31.710
And given that virtually no one reads the
fine print, almost no one knows what’s going

00:07:31.710 --> 00:07:35.000
on until something like this report comes
out.

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:39.550
Considering this, the FTC has required platforms
to be more transparent about their data collection

00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:40.830
and sharing.

00:07:40.830 --> 00:07:44.740
And to BetterHelp’s credit, it seems like
they’ve already implemented this.

00:07:44.740 --> 00:07:48.720
If you go to BetterHelp’s website now, you’re
immediately greeted with this banner on the

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:54.150
bottom that admits that they collect and share
data with Meta and TikTok including personally

00:07:54.150 --> 00:07:55.350
identifiable information.

00:07:55.350 --> 00:08:00.919
Ideally, they wouldn’t share this data at
all but atleast now, they’re being transparent

00:08:00.919 --> 00:08:01.919
about it.

00:08:01.919 --> 00:08:06.810
Moving onto the 4th reform, the FTC is ordering
platforms to be more transparent about how

00:08:06.810 --> 00:08:10.040
encryption doesn’t necessarily protect privacy.

00:08:10.040 --> 00:08:14.491
Here’s the thing, when people here that
their personal data is being encrypted, they

00:08:14.491 --> 00:08:18.139
automatically equate that to their personal
data being protected.

00:08:18.139 --> 00:08:23.509
But, this is a rather misleading statement
because if you give Facebook the encrypted

00:08:23.509 --> 00:08:29.120
data along with the keys to decrypt it, while
the data is still being kept secure, it’s

00:08:29.120 --> 00:08:31.100
not exactly being kept private.

00:08:31.100 --> 00:08:33.800
Next up, the 5th reform is pretty straight
forward.

00:08:33.800 --> 00:08:37.960
The FTC wants these platforms to play a more
active role in monitoring what data their

00:08:37.960 --> 00:08:40.800
ad partners actually collect and share.

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:45.800
And finally, the last reform prevents platforms
from misleadingly using certain seals in their

00:08:45.800 --> 00:08:47.350
advertising material.

00:08:47.350 --> 00:08:52.089
For example, BeterHelp allegedly used the
term HIPAA throughout their marketing material,

00:08:52.089 --> 00:08:56.149
something that likely conveyed that some sort
of 3rd party or government agency had reviewed

00:08:56.149 --> 00:08:59.700
and certified the platform when that wasn’t
the case.

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:04.529
Honestly, all of these reforms are great steps
forward in making online therapy a truly safe

00:09:04.529 --> 00:09:05.810
and private space.

00:09:05.810 --> 00:09:11.079
But, I think this still leaves one overarching
question unanswered.

00:09:11.079 --> 00:09:14.220
Should we even be promoting online therapy?

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:24.880
THE PROBLEM WITH ONLINE THERAPY:
It’s no wonder why online therapy has exploded

00:09:24.880 --> 00:09:28.070
in popularity especially with the pandemic.

00:09:28.070 --> 00:09:31.430
People like the convenience of being able
to talk to a professional from the comfort

00:09:31.430 --> 00:09:32.820
of their home.

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:38.260
But the simple reality is that online therapy
doesn’t stack up to in person therapy and

00:09:38.260 --> 00:09:41.450
I don’t think that’s really a controversial
statement.

00:09:41.450 --> 00:09:45.860
The in person connection is simply on a different
level which generally leads to far better

00:09:45.860 --> 00:09:50.430
results but there’s more to consider than
just results.

00:09:50.430 --> 00:09:55.370
One of the biggest shortfalls of online therapy
platforms like BetterHelp is that they don’t

00:09:55.370 --> 00:09:57.020
accept health insurance.

00:09:57.020 --> 00:10:01.560
This means that the entire cost of the platform
and therapist is coming out of your pocket.

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:06.050
To BetterHelp’s credit, they have kept pricing
quite affordable all things considered.

00:10:06.050 --> 00:10:08.430
Only $60 to $90 per week.

00:10:08.430 --> 00:10:12.690
But with that being said, if you leverage
your health insurance and go to an in person

00:10:12.690 --> 00:10:17.220
clinic, you should be able to get a far higher
quality of care for the same out of pocket

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:18.220
cost.

00:10:18.220 --> 00:10:23.390
Also, speaking about the quality of care,
in person clinics will almost always have

00:10:23.390 --> 00:10:25.079
higher quality care.

00:10:25.079 --> 00:10:27.279
This isn’t a knock on BetterHelps therapists.

00:10:27.279 --> 00:10:31.940
I’m sure there’s a good number of highly
qualified therapists on BetterHelp, but the

00:10:31.940 --> 00:10:38.220
key thing to note is that therapists on BetterHelp
are psychologists, not psychiatrists.

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:42.380
In fact, BetterHelp does not even currently
support psychiatrists.

00:10:42.380 --> 00:10:46.940
This might sound like a novel difference but
it’s not.

00:10:46.940 --> 00:10:50.660
Psychiatrists are actual medical professionals
who went to medical school and are cerfitied

00:10:50.660 --> 00:10:54.120
to diagnose illness and prescribe medication.

00:10:54.120 --> 00:10:57.430
Psychologists on the other hand are more or
less just professionals that you can talk

00:10:57.430 --> 00:10:58.430
to.

00:10:58.430 --> 00:11:03.230
Again, I’m sure there’s plenty of highly
qualified psychologists but if you need serious

00:11:03.230 --> 00:11:08.190
help including diagnosis, prescriptions, and
professional medical advice, a psychiatrist

00:11:08.190 --> 00:11:09.620
is who you need to see.

00:11:09.620 --> 00:11:14.720
But, I bet most BetterHelp users aren’t
even aware of this distinction or how BetterHelp

00:11:14.720 --> 00:11:18.930
doesn’t have psychiatrists which brings
us back to really the core issue at hand which

00:11:18.930 --> 00:11:21.269
is the commercialization of therapy.

00:11:21.269 --> 00:11:26.810
While platforms like BetterHelp may have the
bets of intentions, they’re also trying

00:11:26.810 --> 00:11:27.990
to maximize profits.

00:11:27.990 --> 00:11:32.610
In an industry like social media, search,
or email, it’s whatever.

00:11:32.610 --> 00:11:36.690
But, when it comes to mental health, this
becomes a lot more dangerous.

00:11:36.690 --> 00:11:42.470
In fact, most of BetterHelp’s shady activity
can be attributed to their profit motive.

00:11:42.470 --> 00:11:45.070
Why does BetterHelp share personal user data
with Meta?

00:11:45.070 --> 00:11:49.320
Well, to reach as many people as possible
and make as much money as possible.

00:11:49.320 --> 00:11:52.430
Why does BetterHelp have arguably misleading
promotional material?

00:11:52.430 --> 00:11:57.070
Well, to reach as many people as possible
and make as much money as possible.

00:11:57.070 --> 00:12:01.280
Why do BetterHelp users not know the difference
between psychologists and psychitraists?

00:12:01.280 --> 00:12:05.800
Well, because in BetterHelp’s pursuit of
reaching as many users as possible and making

00:12:05.800 --> 00:12:10.580
as much money as possible, talking about services
that they don’t offer isn’t exactly in

00:12:10.580 --> 00:12:11.930
their best interest.

00:12:11.930 --> 00:12:14.310
So, why is BetterHelp dangerous?

00:12:14.310 --> 00:12:19.000
Well, because they’re commercializing therapy
like never before and that naturally comes

00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:22.160
along with a bunch of side effects.

00:12:22.160 --> 00:12:24.190
Someone else who has sold their soul is Skillshare.

00:12:24.190 --> 00:12:29.000
Check out this video to learn more.

